Appendix C. LEMMA ON EXTREMUM ESTIMATION
Consider h : S → R to be a bounded function on a compact set S. Let extS(h) be an extremum of h (supremum or infimum), and e ̃xtS(h) denotes the corresponding empirical estimate from N i.i.d samples. We refer ε−optimal region as {x ∈ S|h(S) − h(x) ≤ ε} for the supremum, and {x ∈ S|h(x) − h(S) ≤ ε} for the infimum. A standard result regarding the probabilistic bound on the error of an empirical extremum can derived as follows,
Lemma 3. For any desired tolerance ε > 0 and risk level σ ∈ (0, 1), there is a number N0 = ⌈ log(σ)
log(1−Pε) ⌉ ∈ N such
that, for any N ≥ N0, we have
P |e ̃xtS(h) − extS(h)| ≤ ε ≥ 1 − σ,
where Pε is the probability measure of the corresponding ε−optimal set.
Proof. An estimation error greater than ε occurs if and only if all N i.i.d samples fall outside the ε−optimal region. Bounding this failure probability by σ requires (1 − Pε)N ≤ σ, which yields that N ≥ ⌈ log(σ)
log(1−Pε) ⌉.